# Rust notebook to PDF and transparent PNG

## Scope and existing evidence

Implement page compilation and real PDF/PNG bytes using Rust page, handwriting
and image composition authorities, without invoking the Python prototype.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Verified printed-date partial integration — 2026-11-03

The Rust notebook profile now admits an optional, validated numeric Gregorian
date. The same native paper compositor prints the selected date as fixed-width
vector marks in multi-page PDF and transparent PNG, and refuses impossible
dates or date text without a placeholder. Existing seven-field notebook v1
profiles reopen with an empty date and preserve their previous paper output.

Tests cover native serialization, CLI files, authenticated HTTP, and an actual
Firefox local save/reopen/CLI export. The Firefox test also preserves an exact
Spanish `ñ` statistical override, without claiming a rendered ink glyph.

**Still missing:** body handwriting strokes, complete Spanish/English glyph
shapes and fallbacks, embedded image overlays, and a single saved notebook
containing all content. The output remains a genuine paper layer, not full
notebook content or complete acceptance of P5-011.

## Caller-authored pen-segment overlay (partial) — 2026-11-03

Rust now accepts up to 512 explicit, user-authored physical straight pen
segments with one-based page identity, printable-region endpoint and
stroke-width clearance, RGB ink, and a refusal for invalid page/geometry.
The same native path serialization emits real page-specific vector PDF ink,
and a bounded transparent RGBA rasterizer emits diagonals at the selected
DPI without turning the entire segment bounding box opaque.

A two-page
A4 CLI smoke export (`atrament-profiles notebook --export-paper pdf --pages 2
--ink-line 2 25400 25400 50800 50800 700 '#DE1144'`) passed `pdfinfo`;
selected PNG page 2 has 596 × 842 RGBA pixels at 72 DPI. Test assertions
inspect the decoded PNG pixel channels, PDF page count/path/color, and
explicit refusal of oversize, invalid and off-sheet lines before an existing
file can change. No separate Python runtime or renderer was used.

**Scope:** These are manual caller-selected strokes, not rendered glyphs,
statistically synthesized handwriting or stored notebook content. Notebook
v1 JSON and authenticated localhost profile editor do not persist or expose
these segments. Image overlays, owned glyph shapes/fallbacks, and a saved
two-page content notebook remain unimplemented; this does **not** complete
the P5-011 acceptance criteria.

## Exact authored glyph ink overlay (partial) — 2026-11-03

A bounded UTF-8 text run can now select caller-drawn per-grapheme pen
segments on numbered pages with `--glyph-stroke` and `--place-text`.
The emitted vector PDF and selected-page transparent PNG share the existing
Rust ink path and refuse missing glyph shapes, oversized text/ink, invalid
page geometry or output replacement before writing. A new regression proves
that page-2 glyph ink equals individually specified page-2 pen segments in
both formats and is not silently copied to page 1.

The CLI additionally accepts a separately saved, validated
`atrament.handwriting.statistics.v1` profile for **mean-based** width, height,
ink-thickness, letter-spacing and baseline-offset transformations of the
explicit glyph segments. PDF and PNG share the resulting physical strokes;
unsupported statistical variables remain numeric-only, and output still
requires every visible grapheme to have a caller-authored shape.

There is no saved notebook body-text schema, visual glyph editor, complete
font/stroke set or image layer; these arguments are export-only. This is
not a completed P5-011 end-to-end notebook renderer.

## Acceptance decision

Open. Provide the actual bounded Rust/backend or browser integration and an
accepted/refused example before marking the executable capability complete.
Theoretical design acceptance alone cannot establish that this feature runs.
